    "q-335126": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335126",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "The average distance between the centers of Earth and the Moon is $(3.84\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the Moon is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of the Moon.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\cdot (3.84\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.98\\cdot (3.84)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{20}\\cdot ({10}^{8})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(0.733\\,2…)\\times {10}^{23} \\\\  & =(7.332…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of the Moon is\n$(\\,7.33\\times {10}^{22}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$",
