    "q-335148": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335148",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(1.9\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (9.5\\times {10}^{5})}{1.9\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.2\\cdot 9.5}{1.9}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{5}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =206.77\\times {10}^{3} \\\\  & =20.677\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.677\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.677}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.547…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.5\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
